    "description": "Dasht-e Kavir, commonly called Iran’s Great Salt Desert, is a landscape of saline flats, mudflats, shallow waters, and exposed geological structures on the Iranian Plateau. Ancient evaporite deposits and present-day wind and water processes contribute to its varied appearance.",
    "tags": ["Dasht-e Kavir", "Salt desert", "Iranian Plateau", "Evaporites", "Dust"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:50:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:50:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Dasht-e Kavir: Iran’s Great Salt Desert | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand the salt flats, buried evaporites, mudflats, and windblown dust that distinguish Dasht-e Kavir on the Iranian Plateau."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "salt-desert",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A desert with water in its story",
        "content": "Dasht-e Kavir lies on the Iranian Plateau southeast of Tehran. Its familiar English name, the Great Salt Desert, captures an important feature but can obscure the variety of its surfaces. NASA’s Landsat descriptions identify shallow lakes, mudflats, and salt marshes among its sinuous valleys. This is a desert whose appearance cannot be explained by sand alone. [s1][s2]\n\nThe broader Kavir and Lut freshwater ecoregion consists of closed drainage basins. Water drains toward terminal depressions rather than continuing through a river system to the sea. Such a setting makes the relationship between surrounding uplands and low basin floors central to understanding the landscape. The ecoregion is a broader analytical unit, however, and should not be treated as an exact boundary for Dasht-e Kavir itself. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "buried-salt",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An older landscape beneath the surface",
        "content": "Some of the desert’s most conspicuous patterns record a much older environmental history. NASA’s geological interpretation of Landsat imagery describes ancient marine inundation and evaporation as the origin of substantial buried salt deposits. Those deposits subsequently became involved in deformation beneath younger rocks. This account concerns the geological history of the basin; it does not mean that all salt visible today formed in a single episode. [s4]\n\nSalt can behave differently from its rocky overburden. Under suitable conditions, buried salt moves upward through softer material, producing structures called diapirs or salt domes. Erosion can then remove their upper parts and expose patterns within them. NASA also identifies later folding and faulting in the photographed landscape, showing that salt movement and subsequent tectonic deformation have left overlapping signatures. A satellite view therefore combines evidence of deposition, burial, movement, and erosion. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "western-margin",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The margins differ from the basin floor",
        "content": "The western margin provides a useful reminder that the Great Salt Desert is not a uniform expanse. NASA’s account of Kavir National Park describes rocky uplands, desert scrub, and fans of sediment spreading outward from the foot of higher ground. These alluvial fans are deposits carried from uplands by flowing water. Their presence makes water’s role visible even where the overall landscape appears dry. [s5]\n\nThe same account distinguishes the park from the neighboring Salt Lake, or Daracheh-ye Namak, immediately outside its boundaries. This distinction matters when interpreting photographs or reading descriptions: a protected area, an adjacent salt pan, and the wider desert are different geographic entities. The historical satellite account documents their spatial relationship, rather than establishing their present ecological condition. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "dust-and-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A source of moving sediment",
        "content": "Wind can move material well beyond the surface where it originated. NASA documented dust plumes rising from Dasht-e Kavir in January 2013, and a separate satellite comparison showed a storm in November 2017 carrying material eastward. The later account identifies the western basin, with its ephemeral lakes and marshes, as a typical source of dust. These observations connect apparently static salt-desert scenery with active atmospheric transport. [s1][s6]\n\nIndividual images establish that particular events occurred; they do not establish whether storms are becoming more frequent. The most useful way to read Dasht-e Kavir is consequently at several timescales: ancient deposits explain part of its geological structure, intermittent water shapes its lowlands and margins, and wind can redistribute exposed sediment during a single storm. Each process contributes to the desert’s changing surface. [s4][s5][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4", "s5", "s6"]
      }
    ],
